00:19:01,512 --> 00:19:02,134
Scott: There is a news.

283
00:19:02,194 --> 00:19:07,250
Scott: So we all talked about this, probably, but, you know, it's worth talking about again.

284
00:19:07,430 --> 00:19:12,849
Scott: So the European Union passed a law and the law is basically like, hey, it's basically the Apple law.

285
00:19:14,111 --> 00:19:15,454
Rym: It's a few other companies.

286
00:19:15,614 --> 00:19:18,321
Rym: It is the Digital Markets Act.

287
00:19:18,642 --> 00:19:20,005
Rym: The European Parliament passed it in 2022.

288
00:19:20,005 --> 00:19:20,747
Rym: It's going into effect.

289
00:19:22,451 --> 00:19:23,213
Rym: It's complicated.

290
00:19:23,253 --> 00:19:29,270
Rym: We're not experts in all of its deal, but I'll link to a Wikipedia article and also the European Commission.

291
00:19:29,330 --> 00:19:34,669
Rym: It has a really nice website that explains why they passed this act and what they intended to do.

292
00:19:35,611 --> 00:19:35,771
Scott: Right.

293
00:19:35,791 --> 00:19:42,030
Scott: So the intent of the law was basically like, hey, you know, you've created a digital marketplace, a platform of some kind.

294
00:19:42,110 --> 00:19:45,728
Scott: You can't just seal it up and make a walled garden like Apple App Store.

295
00:19:45,869 --> 00:19:46,050
Scott: Right.

296
00:19:46,130 --> 00:19:48,377
Scott: You have to let other people have app stores on there.

297
00:19:48,657 --> 00:19:52,348
Scott: You have to let other people use their other payment platforms of choice.

298
00:19:52,889 --> 00:19:53,090
Scott: Right.

299
00:19:53,451 --> 00:19:55,996
Scott: You can't just, you know, seal it up.

300
00:19:56,236 --> 00:19:56,477
Scott: Right.

301
00:19:57,038 --> 00:20:03,110
Scott: And so it's like basically, you know, when everyone read this, it's like, oh, Apple's going to have to let everyone sideload apps.

302
00:20:03,351 --> 00:20:04,094
Scott: They got no choice.

303
00:20:04,114 --> 00:20:04,435
Scott: All right.

304
00:20:04,455 --> 00:20:06,041
Scott: They're going to have to let other app stores in.

305
00:20:06,061 --> 00:20:06,845
Scott: They got no choice.

306
00:20:06,905 --> 00:20:08,210
Scott: The other I guess they do have a choice.

307
00:20:08,270 --> 00:20:15,390
Scott: The choice would be don't sell Apple products in Europe, which that's a too big a money loss for them to turn away from.

308
00:20:15,570 --> 00:20:30,770
Scott: So Apple basically complied, as far as we can tell, with the letter of the law, basically the way someone would if they were trying to win at a game and complying with the rules.

309
00:20:30,910 --> 00:20:31,413
Scott: Right.

310
00:20:31,715 --> 00:20:34,590
Scott: But not complying with the spirit in any way whatsoever.

311
00:20:34,970 --> 00:20:40,930
Rym: The treaty said I couldn't use chemical gases, but it didn't say I couldn't use liquid chemicals.

312
00:20:43,591 --> 00:20:43,892
Scott: Right.

313
00:20:43,932 --> 00:20:44,213
Scott: Yes.

314
00:20:44,414 --> 00:20:45,739
Scott: I cannot poison you.

315
00:20:45,759 --> 00:20:46,842
Scott: I didn't poison you.

316
00:20:46,863 --> 00:20:48,468
Scott: I shot you.

317
00:20:51,570 --> 00:21:00,128
Scott: So some of the some of the issues that have come up, Apple's like, all right, you want to put out, you know, you want to use a Web browser with an actual different engine.

318
00:21:00,168 --> 00:21:00,890
Scott: that's not Safari.

319
00:21:01,691 --> 00:21:02,413
Scott: You can do that.

320
00:21:02,493 --> 00:21:05,763
Scott: You can get your Firefox and your Chrome on your iPhone.

321
00:21:06,004 --> 00:21:06,505
Scott: Go ahead.

322
00:21:06,586 --> 00:21:07,990
Scott: However, right.

323
00:21:09,390 --> 00:21:18,150
Scott: Number one, we got this rule for everybody, but it's especially going to hurt the browsers is if you get too many installs, you're going to have to pay us money.

324
00:21:18,610 --> 00:21:18,771
Rym: Right.

325
00:21:18,791 --> 00:21:20,195
Scott: We're going to charge you a fee.

326
00:21:20,536 --> 00:21:25,350
Scott: They didn't say we couldn't charge you a fee through, like, you know, using our technology or whatever.

327
00:21:25,990 --> 00:21:30,722
Scott: So if you get a million people to install Chrome or Firefox, you have to pay us like a small fortune.

328
00:21:30,742 --> 00:21:34,150
Scott: And it's like, well, I guess how are we going to be able to do that?

329
00:21:34,350 --> 00:21:35,713
Scott: We don't have any fucking money.

330
00:21:37,256 --> 00:21:39,560
Scott: And two, there's a there's a feature.

331
00:21:39,761 --> 00:21:44,370
Scott: You have this on your phone now if you have iPhone or Android called Progressive Web Apps.

332
00:21:44,490 --> 00:21:54,730
Scott: And what this is, is you can go to any Web site, basically any Web site, including like our Web site and add the Web site to your home screen of your phone, whatever phone you have.

333
00:21:54,790 --> 00:21:59,803
Scott: And what that will do is on your home screen of your phone, there'll be an app.

334
00:22:00,424 --> 00:22:02,770
Scott: But that app is actually just a Web site like you.

335
00:22:03,010 --> 00:22:05,435
Scott: It'll look like an app that have its own icon.

336
00:22:05,776 --> 00:22:06,337
Scott: You touch it.

337
00:22:06,858 --> 00:22:10,265
Scott: A thing opens up that looks like a standalone app.

338
00:22:10,605 --> 00:22:12,850
Scott: There's no URL bar or anything on there.

339
00:22:12,970 --> 00:22:13,210
Scott: Right.

340
00:22:13,871 --> 00:22:19,959
Scott: But it's just a Web site and all Android iPhones can do this, do progressive Web apps.

341
00:22:20,039 --> 00:22:27,990
Scott: And it basically allows developers to make Web pages that are apps that people can install quotes on their phone.

342
00:22:28,671 --> 00:22:33,464
Scott: You know, it just without any app stores or any money or any anything.

343
00:22:33,664 --> 00:22:35,549
Scott: you can do this now, even on iPhones.

344
00:22:35,750 --> 00:22:35,890
Scott: Right.

345
00:22:35,970 --> 00:22:41,910
Scott: And it's actually Apple's argument that they're not closed because you can do that instead if you don't want to be in our app store.

346
00:22:42,070 --> 00:22:43,332
Scott: Right.

347
00:22:44,133 --> 00:22:56,770
Scott: And so what they did is like, well, since people have these other Web browsers on there, right, instead of just Safari, if someone wants to do a progressive Web app, we got to be like, hey, sorry.

348
00:22:57,171 --> 00:23:03,249
Scott: But, you know, from now on, you know, the progressive Web apps aren't going to be all nice and integrated like they're supposed to be.

349
00:23:03,771 --> 00:23:07,098
Scott: They're just going to open in the default browser that you've chosen.

350
00:23:07,138 --> 00:23:09,182
Scott: You want to allow people to use different browsers.

351
00:23:09,623 --> 00:23:13,010
Scott: That means that we can't guarantee progressive Web app stuff.

352
00:23:13,631 --> 00:23:22,010
Scott: So we're going to make an annoying pop up and, you know, you're going to and then it's going to open in your other browser with the URL bar and not integrated nicely or anything.

353
00:23:23,351 --> 00:23:28,542
Scott: And so it's like they're basically saying, hey, we had a way for you to install apps that weren't in the app store.

354
00:23:29,023 --> 00:23:32,250
Scott: And now you want us to support this other way of sideloading.

355
00:23:32,591 --> 00:23:39,070
Scott: We're going to take away the progressive Web app way, effectively speaking, and basically make it, you know, right.

356
00:23:41,031 --> 00:23:46,990
Scott: And then the final thing is they're like, hey, all right, you make your own separate app store, right, with your own apps in it.

357
00:23:47,511 --> 00:23:49,075
Scott: And, you know, we can't deny you.

358
00:23:49,275 --> 00:23:55,710
Scott: We can't say they're not approved or whatever, but we can put all sorts of scary messages that are like, hey, don't do this.

359
00:23:55,990 --> 00:23:57,855
Scott: You know, you might mess up your phone.

360
00:23:57,916 --> 00:24:01,566
Scott: security, privacy, you know, don't do these things.

361
00:24:01,626 --> 00:24:02,107
Scott: Warning.

362
00:24:02,428 --> 00:24:02,689
Scott: Right.

363
00:24:02,749 --> 00:24:02,970
Scott: Right.

364
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:05,418
Scott: We're going to scare you away from giving anyone else money.

365
00:24:05,438 --> 00:24:09,250
Scott: And also anyone who does that has to give us money.

366
00:24:11,291 --> 00:24:15,949
Scott: It's like we're going to charge you a fee for doing this nonsense.

367
00:24:16,150 --> 00:24:16,270
Scott: Right.

368
00:24:16,310 --> 00:24:17,032
Scott: You can't escape.

369
00:24:17,112 --> 00:24:20,959
Scott: You know, it's like you can escape our app store, but you can't escape giving us money.

370
00:24:21,039 --> 00:24:25,929
Scott: You have to give us a cut because you're using our technologies as part of your apps.

371
00:24:26,190 --> 00:24:26,370
Scott: Right.

372
00:24:26,490 --> 00:24:29,140
Scott: Your apps are relying on technology that we built.

373
00:24:30,525 --> 00:24:31,590
Scott: And so you must give us money.

374
00:24:33,631 --> 00:24:36,679
Rym: I feel like the fundamental issue is I've been thinking about this a lot.

375
00:24:36,699 --> 00:24:40,710
Rym: You know, I'm a product manager, like thinking about this kind of thing is literally my professional job.

376
00:24:41,890 --> 00:24:56,709
Rym: And the more I think about it, I think a fundamental problem is that Apple, as an example, but there are other companies that have this exact same sort of monetization model, the model of we have an ecosystem and we charge people to be in the store.

377
00:24:56,749 --> 00:24:57,870
Rym: that's part of our ecosystem.

378
00:24:58,291 --> 00:25:02,419
Rym: Now, Valve has a similar situation, let's say.

379
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:07,390
Rym: They're just a less evil company and they've milked it less in some regards with an asterisk.

380
00:25:09,491 --> 00:25:23,610
Rym: Apple and companies like them tend to prop up all the other parts of their company from the revenue they get from a small number of parts of the company, usually the app store ecosystem plus the advertising ecosystem.

381
00:25:24,030 --> 00:25:41,170
Rym: So they have these small parts of the company that basically fund the rest of the company, which means they're basically making all the people who happen to just make apps and need them to appear on an iPhone to subsidize all the other businesses that they're in that have nothing to do with apps or even iPhones in many cases.

382
00:25:43,891 --> 00:25:49,410
Rym: And this really is just an artifact of companies are too big and are doing too many things.

383
00:25:49,770 --> 00:26:01,390
Rym: And the bigger you are or the more things you do, unless you're willing to break yourself up, I feel like there have to be limits on like what parts of your company like are allowed to make how much money.

384
00:26:01,691 --> 00:26:06,990
Rym: There should be caps on how much money or revenue Apple is allowed to get from their app store.

385
00:26:07,150 --> 00:26:09,890
Rym: They should have to go to the government and ask to be able to get more.

386
00:26:09,950 --> 00:26:17,370
Scott: So governments, you know, in the time of, you know, we have technology moving so fastly and we have governments moving too slowly.

387
00:26:17,650 --> 00:26:18,272
Scott: It's slow.

388
00:26:18,312 --> 00:26:24,130
Scott: Government was a good feature back in the day when we were trying to get away from kings that would make rash, quick decisions.

389
00:26:24,711 --> 00:26:24,992
Scott: Right.

390
00:26:25,012 --> 00:26:28,990
Rym: We're trying to make an Internet where we can just pass MP3s around and chat about.

391
00:26:29,171 --> 00:26:29,432
Scott: Right.

392
00:26:29,633 --> 00:26:32,390
Scott: But it's like when the world is changing too quickly.

393
00:26:32,812 --> 00:26:33,094
Scott: Right.

394
00:26:33,215 --> 00:26:35,110
Scott: It's like democracy is too slow.

395
00:26:35,530 --> 00:26:35,732
Scott: Right.

396
00:26:35,752 --> 00:26:38,290
Scott: Regulation is too slow and enforcement is too slow.

397
00:26:38,971 --> 00:26:44,079
Scott: And it's like we need to just be like, hey, you know, if Apple does this, it shouldn't.

398
00:26:44,159 --> 00:26:51,550
Scott: it should take like less than a day for like some judge in Europe to say, clearly, that's not the spirit of the law and harsh immediate consequence.

399
00:26:51,770 --> 00:26:55,067
Scott: No iPhones, all Apple stores in Europe close until you do it right.

400
00:26:55,128 --> 00:26:55,469
Scott: Fuck you.

401
00:26:55,810 --> 00:26:56,739
Rym: And they kind of could.

402
00:26:56,759 --> 00:26:56,981
Scott: Right.

403
00:26:57,304 --> 00:26:58,030
Rym: They kind of could.

404
00:26:58,310 --> 00:26:58,773
Scott: They can't do it.

405
00:26:58,874 --> 00:27:01,530
Scott: I'm talking like it's like with the immediacy of a king.

406
00:27:01,671 --> 00:27:01,892
Scott: Right.

407
00:27:01,912 --> 00:27:05,890
Scott: Like, you know, obviously there was a democratically passed law to support this.

