00:28:59,390 --> 00:29:01,240
Rym: There's a Supreme Court case in the United States.

453
00:29:01,260 --> 00:29:02,949
Rym: I'm going to be very brief about this.

454
00:29:03,850 --> 00:29:14,290
Rym: It's effectively, the Republican Party is suing, saying regulations can never be enforced except by the exact letter of the law that created them.

455
00:29:15,231 --> 00:29:20,029
Rym: But the way regulation has worked for all of American history until this Supreme Court lawsuit now...

456
00:29:20,531 --> 00:29:25,669
Scott: Is that they have a law that says, "We write a law that says experts at the FCC will regulate

457
00:29:25,869 --> 00:29:25,889
Scott: X.".

458
00:29:26,593 --> 00:29:33,902
Rym: And then the commission regulates X. So they'll say things like, "Here are some specific rules that are passed by Congress.

459
00:29:34,103 --> 00:29:35,186
Rym: Here are some broader rules.

460
00:29:35,426 --> 00:29:35,808
Rym: Here are some

461
00:29:35,868 --> 00:29:36,469
Rym: intentions.".

462
00:29:36,730 --> 00:29:44,450
Rym: But then government agencies under the executive interpret, well, they interpret and enforce those and use their own intelligent discretion.

463
00:29:44,650 --> 00:29:46,054
Scott: It's called delegation.

464
00:29:46,114 --> 00:29:52,670
Scott: The law delegated the power of Congress to this other group, and that other group wields that power as they see fit.

465
00:29:53,070 --> 00:29:57,570
Scott: And if Congress doesn't see them doing a good job, Congress can undelegate.

466
00:29:57,910 --> 00:30:06,750
Rym: It's also checks and balances, though, because Congress can undelegate, redelegate, or change the rules to make the boundaries of what the agency can do tighter or looser.

467
00:30:06,750 --> 00:30:15,070
Scott: But if Congress cannot delegate power to someone else to enforce or, you know, make laws, then you can't even delegate power to police to do any lying.

468
00:30:15,310 --> 00:30:17,628
Rym: Yeah, so this is basically what the Republicans are doing in the U.S.

469
00:30:17,688 --> 00:30:17,910
Rym: right now.

470
00:30:18,090 --> 00:30:26,990
Rym: They're trying to say that the only regulation that will ever exist is explicit regulations encoded in laws passed by Congress.

471
00:30:27,291 --> 00:30:33,270
Rym: And they're doing this because no regulation could ever pass the filibuster, and they're trying to end the concept of regulation.

472
00:30:34,157 --> 00:30:34,650
Rym: Yeah, right.

473
00:30:34,930 --> 00:30:35,768
Rym: Yeah, like they do.

