00:32:12,198 --> 00:32:12,810
Rym: So what do you got?

492
00:32:13,610 --> 00:32:20,990
Scott: So here is a great demonstration of how to write rules that are airtight and won't be subjected to malicious compliance.

493
00:32:20,990 --> 00:32:30,544
Rym: Which in fact, I want to point out, your thing of the day, this is directly the same thing that we said in our panel on worker placement games than the definitions of worker placement.

494
00:32:30,825 --> 00:32:34,090
Rym: You must be descriptive rather than prescriptive.

495
00:32:34,551 --> 00:32:34,711
Scott: Right.

496
00:32:35,012 --> 00:32:38,760
Scott: So basically the idea is that, let's say that there's a park, right?

497
00:32:38,800 --> 00:32:43,070
Scott: The kind that you play in with, you know, trees and, you know, grass and whatever, right?

498
00:32:43,530 --> 00:32:50,508
