621
00:41:27,605 --> 00:41:29,730
Scott: But then I wanted to play video games.

622
00:41:30,130 --> 00:41:33,255
Scott: So at my house, I got x86 computers.

623
00:41:34,096 --> 00:41:43,610
Scott: And for all the years since the mid 90s, I used x86 computers with Windows of some kind.

624
00:41:43,670 --> 00:41:48,737
Scott: Microsoft operating systems and Linux operating systems, but still PCs.

625
00:41:49,258 --> 00:41:49,458
Scott: Right.

626
00:41:49,919 --> 00:41:50,800
Scott: And that's what I used.

627
00:41:51,401 --> 00:41:57,830
Scott: And even as Macs became popular and I saw other developers using them, I did not use them.

628
00:41:58,371 --> 00:41:59,473
Scott: Right.

629
00:41:59,713 --> 00:42:03,080
Scott: Mostly because, hey, those things don't play games or run.

630
00:42:03,321 --> 00:42:04,223
Scott: You know, they don't.

631
00:42:04,263 --> 00:42:07,750
Scott: they don't do what I need to do, even though I see some benefits to them.

632
00:42:08,170 --> 00:42:09,092
Scott: That is not my deal.

633
00:42:09,933 --> 00:42:21,370
Scott: And then at a job I had in the late 2000s where I did not get to choose my computer, they gave me a Macintosh computer running whatever Mac OS there was at the time.

634
00:42:22,592 --> 00:42:25,960
Scott: And I was unhappy, to say the least.

635
00:42:26,902 --> 00:42:30,150
Scott: It was difficult to do my work with that computer.

636
00:42:31,950 --> 00:42:44,768
Rym: Let's go back to June 19th, 2006, an episode that went up shortly before midnight instead of the normal time, because I had switched to Mac and tried to make GeekNights on a 2006 era Mac.

637
00:42:46,090 --> 00:42:48,414
Scott: And that made so much sense then on paper.

638
00:42:48,514 --> 00:42:48,714
Scott: Right.

639
00:42:49,135 --> 00:42:50,357
Scott: A, we didn't have a lot of money.

640
00:42:50,978 --> 00:42:53,582
Scott: B, it was very small and inexpensive.

641
00:42:53,622 --> 00:42:54,884
Rym: The Mac Mini had just come out.

642
00:42:54,944 --> 00:42:58,850
Rym: And back then, like, I don't think many of you remember what computers were like back then.

643
00:42:59,471 --> 00:43:01,914
Scott: That was like the first Mac Mini with a core solo.

644
00:43:03,896 --> 00:43:08,703
Scott: It had, you know, whatever the things we actually needed to do.

645
00:43:09,043 --> 00:43:11,346
Scott: Browsing the web, making a podcast.

646
00:43:11,406 --> 00:43:11,606
Scott: Right.

647
00:43:11,646 --> 00:43:14,390
Scott: Could theoretically be accomplished with that computer.

648
00:43:14,791 --> 00:43:24,210
Scott: It came with GarageBand, which we had never used, but it looked like a really polished piece of software, not like the Garbo ReSound we were using on Linux or whatever.

649
00:43:24,951 --> 00:43:27,196
Rym: I just want to point out, both our things of the day are gone.

