00:43:27,296 --> 00:43:33,430
Rym: And the news on that episode was Blu-ray debuts and Craigslist has a whole bunch of new features.

651
00:43:36,390 --> 00:43:36,871
Scott: Yep.

652
00:43:37,011 --> 00:43:40,658
Scott: And so we got that core solo and actually, no, it was not a good time.

653
00:43:41,379 --> 00:43:44,565
Rym: It was so bad, but we turned it into a cheap PC, so it worked out in the end.

654
00:43:44,585 --> 00:43:44,805
Scott: It was.

655
00:43:44,885 --> 00:43:47,510
Scott: It served well as an HDPC for many years.

656
00:43:47,650 --> 00:43:49,093
Scott: So it was.

657
00:43:49,154 --> 00:43:50,236
Scott: it was not a waste of money.

658
00:43:50,256 --> 00:43:56,189
Rym: I wish I hadn't taken apart my regular computer because I had to put it back together to do the show that night.

659
00:44:00,351 --> 00:44:01,994
Scott: OK, so it was not a good.

660
00:44:02,355 --> 00:44:09,430
Scott: after the sad Mac experiences, even though other people were using them somehow, I was just like, clearly, that's not for me.

661
00:44:10,030 --> 00:44:10,291
Scott: Right.

662
00:44:10,651 --> 00:44:16,540
Scott: I'll stay away, even though I was all in on iPods and iPhones and iPads.

663
00:44:17,041 --> 00:44:17,281
Scott: Right.

664
00:44:17,662 --> 00:44:18,183
Scott: Which are great.

665
00:44:18,603 --> 00:44:22,930
Scott: I had no problems with those because I was never trying to make them do anything that they didn't do.

666
00:44:23,571 --> 00:44:25,373
Scott: I did things the way they wanted to do them.

667
00:44:25,834 --> 00:44:26,075
Scott: Right.

668
00:44:27,116 --> 00:44:36,310
Scott: But it was always kind of sad that the integration right between the iOS and Windows was not so great, even though they're literally improving it like right now.

669
00:44:36,710 --> 00:44:43,441
Scott: It's like Microsoft and Apple finally got their shit together regarding the integration between iPhones and Windows.

670
00:44:43,642 --> 00:44:48,550
Scott: And it is now like decent, acceptable, which it has never been before.

671
00:44:49,531 --> 00:44:52,415
Scott: So anyway, it comes time where I need a new computer.

672
00:44:52,435 --> 00:44:54,799
Scott: that the one I had was was looking slow.

673
00:44:55,179 --> 00:44:55,460
Scott: Right.

674
00:44:55,780 --> 00:45:01,950
Scott: I was having issues, but I'm like, you know, I could just get another PC.

675
00:45:02,550 --> 00:45:02,791
Scott: Right.

676
00:45:03,232 --> 00:45:04,234
Scott: That's faster and better.

677
00:45:05,095 --> 00:45:06,598
Scott: But I really want to do that right now.

678
00:45:07,039 --> 00:45:08,161
Scott: It's kind of expensive.

679
00:45:08,682 --> 00:45:12,930
Scott: I'd rather get like a 50, 80, 60, you know, right down the line.

680
00:45:13,230 --> 00:45:14,612
Scott: I don't want a 40, whatever.

681
00:45:14,773 --> 00:45:16,355
Scott: I have a 1070.

682
00:45:16,355 --> 00:45:20,000
Scott: Every game I play can play on this computer just fine.

683
00:45:20,140 --> 00:45:26,449
Scott: There is no game out there that requires more performance than this computer does.

684
00:45:26,469 --> 00:45:27,070
Scott: Right.

685
00:45:27,890 --> 00:45:28,993
Rym: That Scott wants to play.

686
00:45:29,734 --> 00:45:30,476
Scott: That I want to play.

687
00:45:30,957 --> 00:45:31,157
Scott: Right.

688
00:45:31,638 --> 00:45:33,041
Scott: So why would I why would I do that?

689
00:45:33,482 --> 00:45:33,723
Scott: Right.

690
00:45:34,464 --> 00:45:36,068
Scott: Whereas the Mac.

691
00:45:36,749 --> 00:45:36,970
Scott: Right.

692
00:45:37,350 --> 00:45:41,997
Scott: Has several benefits that I can actually, you know, see clearly.

693
00:45:42,298 --> 00:45:45,583
Scott: and but why do I trust the Mac after this time?

694
00:45:45,643 --> 00:45:50,630
Scott: Well, when I got my newest job, right, that the previous job, I was using the Surface Pro.

695
00:45:51,131 --> 00:45:52,453
Scott: Right.

696
00:45:52,473 --> 00:45:53,214
Scott: And what?

697
00:45:53,595 --> 00:45:53,795
Scott: to?

698
00:45:54,236 --> 00:45:57,621
Scott: several jobs ago, I had a Surface Pro I was very happy with.

699
00:45:57,641 --> 00:46:02,650
Scott: Then shortly before leaving my previous job, I convinced them to get me the Surface Pro.

700
00:46:03,151 --> 00:46:04,473
Scott: And so I would be happy again.

701
00:46:04,493 --> 00:46:09,662
Scott: And I know and I saw, oh, no, the Surface Pro is not as good as it was before.

702
00:46:09,823 --> 00:46:10,524
Scott: This is sad.

703
00:46:10,544 --> 00:46:13,950
Scott: I do not like this Surface Pro.

704
00:46:14,250 --> 00:46:30,370
Scott: And so when I changed jobs, it happened to be around the time that the M2 MacBook Air came out and I said, all right, I'll go and I'll get an M2 MacBook Pro from the store that I know I can return for no loss of money and try it and see if I could do my job on it.

705
00:46:31,131 --> 00:46:38,900
Scott: And sure enough, I was able to do my job on it because things have changed in the intervening 15 years of time.

706
00:46:39,401 --> 00:46:47,230
Scott: And I said, OK, I would return this and my new employer will buy me the M2 MacBook Air and I will use that for my job.

707
00:46:47,490 --> 00:46:51,536
Scott: And it has been pretty great, actually, for doing my job.

708
00:46:51,556 --> 00:46:52,997
Scott: I don't see any.

709
00:46:53,018 --> 00:46:55,561
Scott: I have no complaint really whatsoever.

710
00:46:57,223 --> 00:47:02,109
Scott: It's like, OK, and it's thin and it's tiny and I can take it with me.

711
00:47:03,211 --> 00:47:05,598
Scott: And yeah, it's.

712
00:47:05,618 --> 00:47:09,270
Scott: there is no complaint whatsoever as far as do my work with the MacBook Air.

713
00:47:10,914 --> 00:47:16,810
Scott: So I'm like, well, you know, even though I also play video games, right.

714
00:47:18,210 --> 00:47:22,948
Scott: The fact is that most of the things I do on my computer are not play video games.

715
00:47:23,289 --> 00:47:23,470
Scott: Right.

716
00:47:24,350 --> 00:47:27,377
Scott: And I use a computer mostly for other things.

717
00:47:27,738 --> 00:47:27,938
Scott: Right.

718
00:47:28,399 --> 00:47:33,330
Scott: You know, photo stuff, programming stuff, podcasting, reading the Internet.

719
00:47:33,991 --> 00:47:34,231
Scott: Right.

720
00:47:34,952 --> 00:47:37,214
Scott: All sorts of other things that are not video game.

721
00:47:38,255 --> 00:47:54,350
Scott: And I'm like, you know, because I'm also using the iOS so much with the integrations and many other reasons, especially mostly the insane speed and performance of the M3 CPU, which are insane.

722
00:47:54,430 --> 00:47:55,212
Scott: Right.

723
00:47:55,913 --> 00:48:05,110
Scott: Maybe I can go to a world where I get that new MacBook Pro and just go with that and don't throw up my desktop.

724
00:48:05,291 --> 00:48:05,471
Scott: Right.

725
00:48:05,592 --> 00:48:10,048
Scott: Obviously, most people are in a spot where they can afford one computer and that's it.

726
00:48:10,068 --> 00:48:10,630
Scott: Right.

727
00:48:11,312 --> 00:48:14,828
Scott: And if that was the case, yeah, I wouldn't get a Mac because I got to play some video games.

728
00:48:16,631 --> 00:48:17,091
Scott: Obviously.

729
00:48:17,112 --> 00:48:17,492
Scott: Yeah.

730
00:48:17,813 --> 00:48:26,090
Scott: But if you can afford to have two computers or you have a computer lying around that can play all the video games you want and you're just going to get an additional computer.

731
00:48:27,631 --> 00:48:29,055
Scott: I'm not missing out on anything.

732
00:48:29,155 --> 00:48:34,350
Rym: I mean, my current old workstation can play Counter-Strike 2 in full glory.

733
00:48:35,050 --> 00:48:37,689
Rym: You can have a pretty weak computer and play a lot of video games.

734
00:48:38,431 --> 00:48:38,631
Scott: Right.

735
00:48:38,751 --> 00:48:41,736
Scott: And plus, there have been many advancements in this field.

736
00:48:41,796 --> 00:48:43,438
Scott: For example, the Steam stream.

737
00:48:44,059 --> 00:48:51,290
Scott: Oftentimes it's like, yeah, the desktop is playing the game, but I'm on my Mac and it's streaming the game to the Mac and it just works.

738
00:48:52,151 --> 00:48:58,730
Scott: And it's like, OK, it's basically like all Steam games run on my Mac because I have a desktop in the house.

739
00:48:59,511 --> 00:48:59,672
Scott: Right.

740
00:48:59,732 --> 00:49:04,380
Scott: And you couldn't tell mostly that it wasn't like running natively or whatever.

741
00:49:05,061 --> 00:49:10,170
Scott: I just open up Steam on the Mac and all these games that are PC only are listed and I can push play and they play.

742
00:49:10,950 --> 00:49:14,235
Scott: And it's like, all right, I'm not missing anything here.

743
00:49:16,658 --> 00:49:25,430
Scott: However, while I am overall satisfied and are not returning the MacBook Pro that I now use as my primary computer.

744
00:49:25,531 --> 00:49:29,210
Rym: Are there perhaps some things in the gears that have ground ever so slightly?

745
00:49:29,550 --> 00:49:29,951
Scott: Oh, yes.

746
00:49:29,971 --> 00:49:34,781
Scott: There is a list of things that are still gripes.

747
00:49:34,841 --> 00:49:36,184
Scott: They're anti gripes.

748
00:49:36,224 --> 00:49:38,970
Scott: There are things the Mac does better, actually, than Windows.

749
00:49:39,470 --> 00:49:47,230
Scott: The way that it handles audio is so much better than the way Windows does it by like a thousand times.

750
00:49:47,330 --> 00:49:55,050
Scott: You can you know, in Windows, you have the situation where like each different stage, there's like a different volume control and they all stack on each other.

751
00:49:55,550 --> 00:49:56,853
Scott: Right.

752
00:49:56,873 --> 00:49:58,916
Scott: In on the Mac, there's always one.

753
00:49:59,517 --> 00:49:59,958
Scott: That is it.

754
00:50:00,619 --> 00:50:00,819
Scott: Right.

755
00:50:01,139 --> 00:50:07,590
Scott: It's like if you've got your AirPods in that, it's like the software control on the Mac is the control for the volume.

756
00:50:08,111 --> 00:50:08,752
Scott: End of story.

757
00:50:09,013 --> 00:50:09,233
Scott: Right.

758
00:50:09,594 --> 00:50:17,030
Scott: If you have an audio external audio interface plugged in, as I do to do this podcast and your headphones plugged into it, the Mac disables.

759
00:50:17,210 --> 00:50:18,995
Scott: It's software audio controls completely.

760
00:50:19,417 --> 00:50:22,706
Rym: So for pro audio interfaces, Windows apps can do that, too.

761
00:50:22,806 --> 00:50:24,129
Rym: And like all Adobe stuff does that.

762
00:50:25,011 --> 00:50:26,716
Scott: But I'm saying like this is the way it does it.

763
00:50:26,837 --> 00:50:27,338
Scott: Period.

764
00:50:27,379 --> 00:50:28,001
Scott: End of story.

765
00:50:28,281 --> 00:50:28,462
Scott: Right.

766
00:50:28,562 --> 00:50:30,308
Scott: It's the basic pro mode all the time.

767
00:50:30,328 --> 00:50:30,910
Scott: Right.

768
00:50:32,850 --> 00:50:34,574
Scott: Also, every individual app.

769
00:50:35,396 --> 00:50:35,636
Scott: Right.

770
00:50:35,997 --> 00:50:38,503
Scott: You can control the volumes individually.

771
00:50:38,523 --> 00:50:39,244
Scott: Right.

772
00:50:39,665 --> 00:50:40,627
Scott: Which Windows?

773
00:50:41,128 --> 00:50:41,810
Scott: not so good at that.

774
00:50:43,771 --> 00:50:45,435
Scott: So there are a lot of other pluses.

775
00:50:45,515 --> 00:50:45,715
Scott: Right.

776
00:50:45,756 --> 00:50:52,049
Scott: Bluetooth things just work more nicely and smoothly and reliably and so forth and so forth.

777
00:50:52,750 --> 00:50:57,879
Scott: But anyway, things that are a problem on the Mac that I'm not happy about.

778
00:50:57,959 --> 00:50:58,240
Scott: Right.

779
00:50:59,081 --> 00:51:01,285
Scott: The biggest one on Windows.

780
00:51:01,606 --> 00:51:03,870
Scott: Let's say you plug a Windows computer into some monitors.

781
00:51:04,411 --> 00:51:04,612
Scott: Right.

782
00:51:05,273 --> 00:51:07,378
Scott: And you can configure it any way you want.

783
00:51:07,398 --> 00:51:11,829
Scott: You can say mirror that screen, extend to that screen, resolution on that screen.

784
00:51:11,849 --> 00:51:12,450
Scott: Right.

785
00:51:12,770 --> 00:51:14,355
Scott: And with Mac, same thing.

786
00:51:14,415 --> 00:51:15,880
Scott: You can configure it however you want.

787
00:51:16,542 --> 00:51:19,370
Scott: But in Windows, there is an additional configuration.

788
00:51:20,191 --> 00:51:22,817
Scott: And that configuration is Windows.

789
00:51:23,158 --> 00:51:28,510
Scott: I know that monitor is plugged into this computer with an HDMI cable, DisplayPort cable, whatever kind of cable.

790
00:51:29,170 --> 00:51:32,940
Scott: However, I would prefer for you to pretend that monitor does not exist.

791
00:51:33,061 --> 00:51:34,344
Scott: Do not send it any signal.

792
00:51:34,886 --> 00:51:35,848
Scott: Do not recognize it.

793
00:51:36,390 --> 00:51:36,530
Scott: Right.

794
00:51:36,810 --> 00:51:37,652
Scott: Do not talk to it.

795
00:51:38,254 --> 00:51:40,841
Scott: And then that monitor will do what it will.

796
00:51:41,101 --> 00:51:43,528
Scott: Probably turn off or search its other inputs.

797
00:51:43,608 --> 00:51:44,430
Scott: for, you know.

798
00:51:44,970 --> 00:51:46,095
Rym: In Windows, you can just do that.

799
00:51:46,115 --> 00:51:48,806
Rym: You just click on a monitor and say it's not a monitor anymore.

800
00:51:48,967 --> 00:51:49,670
Rym: And it's just fine.

801
00:51:49,770 --> 00:51:50,351
Scott: That's correct.

802
00:51:50,932 --> 00:51:52,854
Scott: On the Mac, you cannot do this.

803
00:51:53,955 --> 00:51:55,217
Scott: It's very, very annoying.

804
00:51:55,337 --> 00:51:58,561
Scott: If you want a monitor to switch to another input.

805
00:51:58,641 --> 00:52:06,030
Scott: that is not the Mac, then you must go to that monitor and push buttons on it and tell it to switch to a different input.

806
00:52:07,151 --> 00:52:13,885
Scott: And to make matters worse, it's like, OK, I have chosen this monitor and I have said, monitor, go to a different input.

807
00:52:14,166 --> 00:52:15,990
Scott: There's something else plugged into you besides the Mac.

808
00:52:16,391 --> 00:52:20,379
Scott: I want you to show what's on that other thing and not what the Mac is displaying.

809
00:52:21,020 --> 00:52:21,521
Scott: That works.

810
00:52:22,223 --> 00:52:25,890
Scott: However, the Mac still thinks that monitor is on and displaying.

811
00:52:26,291 --> 00:52:34,608
Scott: So if you had, say, an extended desktop, you must now push extra buttons to say Mac actually mirror that screen.

812
00:52:34,708 --> 00:52:35,750
Scott: And that way it doesn't matter.

813
00:52:36,772 --> 00:52:43,170
Scott: I can't see it because if you're extending to it, obviously there might be apps on it that you can't see because the Mac isn't displaying.

814
00:52:43,510 --> 00:52:44,633
Rym: Your mouse falls into the hole.

815
00:52:44,653 --> 00:52:45,475
Rym: You don't know where it is.

816
00:52:46,498 --> 00:52:47,059
Scott: Exactly.

817
00:52:47,320 --> 00:52:49,425
Scott: So that is extremely annoying.

818
00:52:50,127 --> 00:52:51,170
Scott: I'm upset about that.

819
00:52:51,471 --> 00:52:51,651
Scott: Right.

820
00:52:51,711 --> 00:52:54,680
Scott: It makes me have to push a lot more buttons to switch monitors around.

821
00:52:55,142 --> 00:52:57,770
Scott: For most people who don't have a bunch of monitors, it will never matter.

822
00:52:59,671 --> 00:52:59,931
Scott: All right.

823
00:53:00,011 --> 00:53:00,712
Scott: Second issue.

824
00:53:00,992 --> 00:53:01,233
Scott: Right.

825
00:53:01,693 --> 00:53:14,350
Scott: So on the Macs, there's this natural scrolling feature, which is basically the scrolling direction is reversed with mouse wheels and, you know, when you're on a touch pad, you're swiping your fingers up and down.

826
00:53:14,610 --> 00:53:14,851
Scott: Right.

827
00:53:14,871 --> 00:53:24,990
Scott: Because normally on a Windows PC, right, if you were to scroll and I don't even know if it's up or down, my hand just feels it subconsciously at the correct direction to scroll.

828
00:53:26,830 --> 00:53:27,051
Scott: Right.

829
00:53:27,411 --> 00:53:29,073
Scott: The Mac has it in reverse.

830
00:53:29,954 --> 00:53:32,577
Scott: OK, you can go into the settings and unreversed it.

831
00:53:33,298 --> 00:53:33,799
Scott: Terrific.

832
00:53:34,440 --> 00:53:42,930
Scott: However, sometimes using a touch pad and with a touch pad, you actually kind of want to do it the Mac way, the reversed way.

833
00:53:43,250 --> 00:53:45,334
Scott: That makes sense with a touch pad.

834
00:53:45,554 --> 00:53:45,774
Scott: Right.

835
00:53:45,995 --> 00:53:49,000
Scott: Because it feels like, you know, you're moving the paper or whatever.

836
